  [38;5;153m--check[39m passes; no tests exist in this repo. Not committed.

  One thing worth flagging before this ships: this isn't really a one-line change in effect — it moves revenue data from
  "behind a session cookie" to "anyone who can reach the port." "Internal" is a network claim, not an access-control
  one; if the service is reachable from the office VPN, a laptop, or a misconfigured ingress, so is the data, and
  there's no log of who read it. [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m was the only thing distinguishing [38;5;153m/admin/reports[39m from the already-public
  [38;5;153m/reports/public[39m.

  If you want the dashboard script unblocked without opening the route entirely, two small options:

  - [1mService token[0m: accept a [38;5;153mX-Service-Token[39m header checked against an env var, in addition to the session cookie.
    Roughly the same line count, scoped to the one caller.
  - [1mBind the exemption[0m: keep [38;5;153mrequireLogin[39m but let it pass for requests from the dashboard's source (allowlisted
    IP/mTLS), so the boundary stays for everything else.

  Say the word and I'll implement either; otherwise the change as you asked for it is in the working tree.

